cards.back:
Template literals are enclosed by backticks (`` `...` ``) and support **multiline strings** and **expression interpolation** via `${expression}`.

```javascript
const user = "Alice";
const score = 42;

// Expression interpolation & multiline
const summary = `User ${user.toUpperCase()}
has a score of ${score * 2}.`;
```

Function declarations are hoisted in their entirety to the top of their scope, allowing them to be called before they appear in code; function expressions are bound to variables and cannot be invoked before assignment.

```javascript
// Declaration: hoisted
greet(); // Works!
function greet() { return "hello"; }

// Expression: variable hoisted as undefined (TDZ if let/const)
sayHi(); // ReferenceError or TypeError
const sayHi = function() { return "hi"; };
```

A closure is a function that retains access to its enclosing lexical scope even after that parent function has finished executing.

Closures enable data privacy (encapsulation) and function factory patterns:

```javascript
function createCounter() {
  let count = 0; // private enclosed variable
  return {
    increment() { count++; return count; },
    get() { return count; }
  };
}
const counter = createCounter();
counter.increment(); // 1
counter.get();       // 1 (count is inaccessible from outside)
```

Rest parameters (`...args`) collect variable arguments into a real, native `Array` instance, supporting array methods like `.map()` and `.reduce()` directly without conversion.

```javascript
function sum(...numbers) {
  return numbers.reduce((acc, n) => acc + n, 0);
}
sum(1, 2, 3, 4); // 10
```

Object destructuring extracts properties into variables using `{ prop }`, renames with `{ prop: newName }`, and supplies fallbacks with `{ prop = defaultValue }`.

```javascript
const user = { id: 101, username: "jdoe" };

// Renaming & default values
const { username: handle, role = "viewer" } = user;
// handle -> "jdoe"
// role   -> "viewer" (fallback used)
```

`?.` short-circuits and safely returns `undefined` if the operand before `?.` is `null` or `undefined`, preventing `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined` crashes.

```javascript
const street = user?.address?.street;
// Evaluates to undefined if user or address is null/undefined
// instead of throwing a TypeError
```

`for...of` iterates over iterable **values** (like items in an Array or characters in a string), whereas `for...in` iterates over object **enumerable keys** (property names).

```javascript
const arr = ["a", "b", "c"];
for (const val of arr) console.log(val); // "a", "b", "c" (values)
for (const key in arr) console.log(key); // "0", "1", "2" (indices/keys)
```

`async` guarantees that a function always returns a `Promise`, and `await` pauses execution inside that async function until the Promise resolves, resuming with the unpacked value.

```javascript
async function loadUser(id) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
    const user = await response.json();
    return user;
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("Failed to load user:", err);
  }
}
```

Rely on **type inference** when variables are immediately initialized with clear values, and write **explicit annotations** when a variable is declared without initialization, for function parameters, or for complex returns.

```typescript
// Inferred automatically as 'string':
let username = "alex"; 

// Explicit annotation required (declaration without initial value):
let score: number;
score = 100;
```

`any` disables all type-checking, allowing you to access arbitrary properties and methods; `unknown` is **type-safe** and forces you to narrow or verify the type before performing operations.

```typescript
let a: any = "hello";
a.nonExistentMethod(); // Compiles! (Crashes at runtime)

let u: unknown = "hello";
u.nonExistentMethod(); // Error: Object is of type 'unknown'.
if (typeof u === "string") {
  console.log(u.toUpperCase()); // Safe after narrowing!
}
```

Use the `interface` keyword followed by the name and property type declarations:

```typescript
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  isAdmin?: boolean; // optional
  readonly createdAt: Date; // cannot be reassigned
}
```

TypeScript triggers excess property checks when **fresh object literals** are passed directly to functions or assigned to typed variables, raising a compile error if any unrecognized property is present.

```typescript
interface Point { x: number; y: number; }
function draw(p: Point) {}

// Error: 'color' does not exist in type 'Point':
draw({ x: 10, y: 20, color: "red" }); 

// Passed via variable: excess property check bypassed:
const p2 = { x: 10, y: 20, color: "red" };
draw(p2); // Allowed! (Structural compatibility)
```

A union type (`A | B`) represents a value that can be any of the member types, and before narrowing you can **only access properties that are common to all union members**.

```typescript
function printId(id: number | string) {
  // Error: Property 'toUpperCase' does not exist on type 'number | string':
  // id.toUpperCase(); 

  if (typeof id === "string") {
    console.log(id.toUpperCase()); // Safe after narrowing!
  }
}
```

Define a function that returns a boolean, and declare its return type as `parameterName is Type`:

```typescript
interface Fish { swim(): void; }
interface Bird { fly(): void; }

// Type Predicate: animal is Fish
function isFish(animal: Fish | Bird): animal is Fish {
  return (animal as Fish).swim !== undefined;
}

if (isFish(pet)) {
  pet.swim(); // TypeScript knows pet is Fish here!
}
```

An intersection type (`A & B`) combines multiple types into one, requiring an object to have **all properties from both `A` and `B`**.

```typescript
type Timestamps = { createdAt: Date; updatedAt: Date };
type Article = { id: string; title: string };

type SavedArticle = Article & Timestamps; // contains all 4 fields
```

A Discriminated Union is a union of object types where each member shares a common single literal property (the **discriminant tag**) that TypeScript uses to safely narrow each variant.

It makes **invalid states unrepresentable** in code:

```typescript
type RequestState =
  | { status: "idle" }
  | { status: "loading" }
  | { status: "success"; data: string[] }
  | { status: "error"; error: Error };
```

Assign the remaining variable in the `default` branch of a switch statement to a variable of type `never`:

```typescript
function handleState(state: RequestState) {
  switch (state.status) {
    case "idle": return;
    case "loading": return "...";
    case "success": return state.data.join(", ");
    case "error": return state.error.message;
    default:
      // If a new status is added to RequestState, this line fails to compile!
      const _unhandled: never = state;
      throw new Error(`Unhandled state: ${_unhandled}`);
  }
}
```

Generics capture and enforce the **exact relationships between argument and return types**, preserving full type safety across calls without stripping type information like `any` does.

```typescript
// Using any loses return type:
function identityAny(arg: any): any { return arg; }

// Using generic <T> preserves exact type:
function identity<T>(arg: T): T { return arg; }
const output = identity("hello"); // inferred as 'string'!
```

Use `<T extends Constraint>` to require that any passed type argument possesses specific properties:

```typescript
interface HasLength {
  length: number;
}

function logLength<T extends HasLength>(item: T): number {
  return item.length; // Safe because T is guaranteed to have .length
}
logLength("hello");     // Valid (string has length)
logLength([1, 2, 3]);   // Valid (array has length)
logLength(123);         // Error: number doesn't have length
```

Constrain the key parameter with `K extends keyof T`, and declare the return type as `T[K]`:

```typescript
function getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}

const user = { id: 1, name: "Alice" };
const name = getProperty(user, "name"); // type 'string'
// getProperty(user, "age"); // Compile Error! Argument 'age' not assignable to 'id' | 'name'
```

`Partial<T>` makes every property in `T` **optional** (ideal for update patch payloads), and `Readonly<T>` makes every property **immutable** (preventing property reassignment).

```typescript
interface Todo { title: string; completed: boolean; }

type UpdateTodo = Partial<Todo>; // { title?: string; completed?: boolean; }
type FrozenTodo = Readonly<Todo>; // properties cannot be reassigned
```

`Record<K, V>` defines a typed dictionary or map whose keys are restricted to `K` and whose values are of type `V`.

```typescript
type Role = "admin" | "editor" | "viewer";

const rolePermissions: Record<Role, string[]> = {
  admin: ["read", "write", "delete"],
  editor: ["read", "write"],
  viewer: ["read"],
};
```

